Guided Exercise: Limit Compute Capacity for Applications
Objective
•	Learn how to set memory limits on pods to prevent a buggy application from consuming all node memory.
•	Observe how OpenShift handles pods that exceed memory limits (OOMKilled → pod restart → CrashLoopBackOff).
•	Use monitoring commands to track memory usage and pod status.
________________________________________
2. Key Concepts
Memory Limits
•	Requests → minimum memory for scheduling.
•	Limits → maximum memory a pod can consume.
•	Exceeding limit → Linux OOM killer kills the container process.
•	OpenShift restarts pods automatically (CrashLoopBackOff if repeated).
•	Useful for mitigating memory leaks or buggy applications.
CrashLoopBackOff
•	Occurs when a pod repeatedly crashes.
•	OpenShift adds increasing wait times between restarts.
•	Protects the cluster from continuously failing pods.
________________________________________
3. Steps in the Exercise
a. Setup
lab start reliability-limits

oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
oc project reliability-limits
b. Create Deployment
•	leakapp has a memory leak of 1 MiB/sec.
•	Initial memory request: 20Mi, limit: 35Mi.
oc apply -f ~/DO180/labs/reliability-limits/leakapp.yml
oc get pods
c. Observe Pod Restart due to OOM
watch oc get pods
# Pod restarts every ~30s
Check OOM reason:
oc get pods <pod-name> -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState}' | jq .
# exitCode: 137, reason: "OOMKilled"
•	Pod eventually shows CrashLoopBackOff as OpenShift increases retry wait times.
________________________________________
d. Increase Memory Limit
•	Set a higher limit to allow longer runtime while developers fix the bug:
oc set resources deployment/leakapp --limits memory=600Mi
oc get pods
•	Pod now runs longer without frequent restarts.
________________________________________
e. Monitor Memory Usage
•	Monitor pod memory usage with:
oc adm top pods
watch oc adm top pods
•	When usage approaches 600Mi, the pod is killed and restarted by OOM.
•	Monitor pod status simultaneously:
watch oc get pods
________________________________________
f. Finish Exercise
lab finish reliability-limits
________________________________________
4. Key Observations
1.	OpenShift automatically restarts pods that exceed memory limits.
2.	CrashLoopBackOff delays protect the cluster while buggy pods are repeatedly failing.
3.	Setting appropriate memory requests and limits protects other workloads and nodes.
4.	Use oc adm top pods to track actual memory usage.
________________________________________
✅ Summary Table
Resource	Behavior if exceeded
Memory request	Scheduling only; no runtime enforcement
Memory limit	Pod killed → OOMKilled → restart
CPU limit	Pod throttled; slower execution, no kill

